  "id": "kansai_foku_urc",
  "name": "Kansai foku (Kansai underground folk song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Kansai foku was the protest and topical folk song movement of Osaka and Kyoto in the late 1960s, when singer-songwriters modelled on the American folk revival wrote blunt Japanese songs about war, labour, discrimination and student life. The Underground Record Club (URC), founded in 1969 as a mail-order label, issued songs major labels would not release by Okabayashi Nobuyasu, Takada Wataru and Takaishi Tomoya, and the Nakatsugawa Folk Jamboree became the movement's festival.",
  "recipe": "classic Asian 1967-1969 kansai foku, protest Song, east Asian branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. dreadnought acoustic guitar, harmonica. treated studio. Neumann U47, German V72 tube pre, German tube console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. tape 15ips plate reverb.",
